.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt XFT_PALLOC 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_palloc ,
.Nm xft_palloc_reserve ,
.Nm xft_palloc_commit ,
.Nm xft_palloc_guard ,
.Nm xft_palloc_resize ,
.Nm xft_palloc_free
.Nd page allocator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In alloc.h
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_palloc "t_size size"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve "t_size size"
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_palloc_commit "t_any ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_palloc_guard "t_any ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_palloc_resize "t_buffer b" "t_size new_size"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_palloc_free "t_buffer b"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions take memory directly from the operating system: Linux uses
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr mremap 2 ,
.Xr mprotect 2
and
.Xr munmap 2 ,
and Windows calls
.Ql VirtualAlloc ,
.Ql VirtualProtect
and
.Ql VirtualFree .
The arena, general-purpose and reporting allocators get their memory here, and
.Fn xft_page_allocator
wraps these functions in a
.Vt t_allocator ;
see
.Xr xft_alloc 3 .
The memory is private, anonymous and zero-filled.
.Pp
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
reserves address space for
.Fa size
bytes, and
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
backs the
.Fa size
bytes at
.Fa ptr
with readable and writable memory.
.Fn xft_palloc
does both and releases the reservation if the commit fails.
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
makes the pages of a range inaccessible, so that any access to them faults.
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
grows or shrinks
.Fa b
to
.Fa new_size ,
moving it when needed, and
.Fn xft_palloc_free
releases
.Fa b .
.Ss Sizes
The
.Fa .size
of a returned buffer is the rounded size, and
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
and
.Fn xft_palloc_free
expect the buffer back exactly as returned.
.Pp
On Linux, a size of at most 1 MiB rounds up to the next power of two, and
to at least 8.
A larger size rounds up to the next hugepage tier: 2 MiB, 8 MiB, 16 MiB,
256 MiB, 512 MiB, 1 GiB, 2 GiB and, on 64-bit targets, 16 GiB.
A size beyond the largest tier is kept as is.
.Pp
On Windows, sizes round up to a multiple of 4096, and hugepages are not used.
.Ss Linux
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
maps the memory readable and writable, so a reservation is usable at once and
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
does nothing.
A mapping whose rounded size is a hugepage tier is first requested with
.Dv MAP_HUGETLB
and the matching
.Dv MAP_HUGE_*
size bits.
When that fails, because no huge pages of that size are reserved or the CPU
has no huge pages of that size, the memory is mapped again with ordinary pages,
which transparent huge pages may promote.
x86-64 has 2 MiB and 1 GiB huge pages, so its other tiers always get ordinary
pages.
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
calls
.Xr mremap 2
with
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE ;
a mapping keeps the page size it was created with.
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
calls
.Xr mprotect 2
with
.Dv PROT_NONE ,
so
.Fa ptr
must be aligned to the mapping's page size.
.Ss Windows
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
reserves with
.Dv MEM_RESERVE ,
and nothing in the reservation is accessible until
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
commits it with
.Dv MEM_COMMIT
and
.Dv PAGE_READWRITE .
Both act on every page the range touches.
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
sets
.Dv PAGE_NOACCESS
with
.Ql VirtualProtect ,
which works on committed pages only; reserved pages that were never committed
fault on access anyway.
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
allocates a new buffer, copies the smaller of the two sizes and frees the old
buffer.
.Fn xft_palloc_free
releases the whole reservation with
.Dv MEM_RELEASE ,
whatever
.Fa b Ns .size
holds.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_palloc
and
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
return a buffer whose
.Fa .mem
is
.Dv nullptr
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
returns
.Fa b
unchanged when the rounded
.Fa new_size
equals
.Fa b Ns .size ,
and a buffer with
.Fa .mem
set to
.Dv nullptr
and
.Fa .size
set to 0 on failure, in which case
.Fa b
remains valid.
.Pp
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
and
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
return
.Dv OK ,
or
.Dv KO
on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Place input flush against a guard page, so that reading one byte past it
faults:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_buffer	page;
t_u8		*end;

page = xft_palloc(8192);
if (page.mem == nullptr
	|| !xft_palloc_guard((t_u8 *)page.mem + 4096, 4096))
	xft_exit_process(1);
end = (t_u8 *)page.mem + 4096;
xft_memcpy(end - 3, "abc", 3);
/* end[-3] to end[-1] are readable; end[0] faults */
xft_palloc_free(page);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_arena 3 ,
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
On Linux, the tiers make a request just past one tier reserve the next: 17 MiB
reserves 256 MiB of address space.
With ordinary pages only the pages that are touched take memory; a mapping
backed by huge pages takes them from the reserved pool when it is created.
.Pp
On Linux,
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
fails on a mapping backed by huge pages when the new size is not a multiple of
its huge page size, for example when shrinking a 2 MiB mapping to 1 MiB or
less.
.Pp
On Linux,
.Fn xft_palloc_free
and
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
act on
.Fa b Ns .size
bytes, so a buffer with a different size unmaps or moves a different range.
